// Compiled-coverage ratchet (design plan: "Make the boru bytecode VM fully
// independent of the interpreter at runtime", phase P0). The runtime-
// independence work drives every supported program to compile to bytecode
// that runs entirely in the VM, so that BOTH interpreter dependencies — the
// OpFallback island and the whole-program fallback — can be deleted.
//
// This test is the objective measure of that goal. It runs EVERY spec value
// row through CompileCheck and counts the rows that REFUSE (no Program, no
// check error), bucketed by a normalised refusal reason. The total refusal
// count is a downward ratchet: it must never rise above the recorded ceiling,
// and each phase (P2..P6) lowers the ceiling as a refusal category is
// eliminated. P7 (delete the fallback) is gated on this reaching ZERO.
//
// Rows that error during the check pass itself (parse errors, type-error
// rows) are NOT refusals — the program is statically invalid in both engines
// — and are reported separately, not counted against the ceiling.
package langspec

import (
	"sort"
	"strings"
	"testing"
)

// refusalCeiling is the maximum number of spec value rows allowed to refuse
// compilation (CompileCheck returns a nil Program with no check error AND the
// row is not a statically-invalid check-diagnostics row). The runtime-
// independence phases lower it monotonically; it must reach 0 before the
// interpreter fallback can be deleted (plan P7). Never raise it.
const refusalCeiling = 0 // def-node-binding.tsv:54 (`def c1 1 def mk fn [[c1:Integer] [List] [[c1]]] mk 9` -> `[1]`) now compiles — the LAST refusal, reaching the P7 floor. The body `[[c1]]` returns a deferred list that the interpreter auto-evaluates LATE, in MODULE scope (a returned list never closes over the param — only a `=>` lambda captures), so `c1` resolves to the module binding `1`, not the arg `9`. A fn UNIT cannot model this (its result is fixed at call time and cannot defer to a later module rebind: `mk 9 def c1 2` -> `[2]`). The fix makes the deferred-list-body fn TRANSPARENT: buildFnBodyReturnsFn (core_helpers.go, via deferredParamListResidual) hands the RAW deferred list back as the call's residual instead of compiling a unit, and tryRecordDeferredList (carrier.go) records NOTHING for the dispatch (suppressing the "user fn call Stage 3" refusal) so the list rides as the result and the EXISTING top-level deferred-list fold bakes it in module scope — exactly as a top-level `def c1 1 [[c1]]` already compiles to `PUSH_CONST [[1]]`. Every consumption path folds correctly in the SAME check pass the interpreter mirrors: `mk 9` -> [[1]], rebind `mk 9 def c1 2` -> [[2]], def-bind `def r (mk 9) def c1 2 r` -> [[1]] (def forces the eval at bind time), consume `mk 9 0 get` -> [1] / `mk 9 size` -> [1]. No VM/opcode change; the program bakes the const the fold yields. Differential-clean (TestSpecCompiledDifferential/OrFallback + property fuzz + combinations). 1 -> 0. PRIOR: macro.tsv:45 (recursive macro too-deep) compiles to a TERMINAL OpTrap: `def loopy (macro [[a] [quote [loopy unquote a]]])  macroexpand (loopy 1)` -> a single OpTrap raising the byte-identical `macroexpand_error: macroexpand: expansion too deep (recursive macro?)` the interpreter raises. The PREREQUISITE the prior attempt missed was NOT in the FormArgs paren-capture (that path is sound — the macro-headed paren IS captured raw and macroexpand DOES dispatch); it was the dynamic-help example generator (native_help.go OnRegisterHook) running the recursive `loopy` macro to the step ceiling during `def loopy`, which burned the registry-shared check-mode StepCount (BudgetTripped short-circuits every subsequent sub-engine) so `macroexpand (loopy 1)` was never reached. Fixed by CheckState.IsolateBudget — the FOURTH hermetic channel (alongside IsolateEmit / TruncateDiagnostics / Defs restore) snapshotting+restoring StepCount/BudgetTripped around the synthetic eval. With that, macroexpand reaches carrierResults, ExpandMacroForm runs to the depth guard, and a top-level RecordTrap (declines a nested occurrence, which keeps the lenient fallback — verified) compiles the byte-identical macroexpand_error (mirrors the mini/parse/emit *_unknown_lang traps). Deterministic (20x in-process + across fresh processes: identical single OpTrap; the depth-256 expansion advancing the time-seeded value-ID RNG does not affect the trap). Differential-clean (TestSpecCompiledDifferential/OrFallback) + TestMacroexpandCompilesNative/TestDynamicHelpBudgetIsolation. 2 -> 1. PRIOR: module-test:38 (the boru:test spec runner) compiles: `… s Test.run-spec end Test.summary` -> `{total:2 passed:2 failed:0}`, fully native (run-spec body -> PUSH_CLOSURE test-describe$body, run-cases/run-case -> CALL_USER, test-invoke/test-record -> CALL_NATIVE_POLY). The lever is ZERO-COUNT `for` PRUNING: `run-spec`'s `for (subs size) [subspec run-spec]` over `subs: []` recurses into `run-spec` over a carrier `subspec` that cannot statically dispatch (the recursive `test-describe` over an Any carrier hits "unmatched dispatch recovered"). Both engines run a `for` with a concrete non-positive count ZERO times — body unreachable, zero values, no side effects — so forCarrierAnalyse now PRUNES it: when the count operand is a concrete <=0 Integer (or an empty literal range), it analyses neither the body nor records a loop, returning a 0-value result. The prerequisite that makes `subs size` a concrete 0 is a new `size` ReturnsFn folding a CONCRETE list/map to its static element count (subs folds to concrete [] via the existing getNodeReturns container fold, so `subs size` -> concrete 0). Faithful (interpreter `for 0` is the same no-op) and differential-clean: compiled==interpreter byte-identical INCLUDING the test-record side effects ({total:2 passed:2 failed:0}, never double-counted — test-record's SkipsSideEffect screen suppresses the check pass). Live refused count 2 (def-node-binding:54 operand-provenance + macro:45 residual-not-materialisable — both PROVEN refusals: compiling the guess available today ships a wrong answer — open defects, not exemptions). See TestRunSpecHarnessCompiles + design/legacy/boru-bytecode-stage3-inlining-plan.0.ignore. 3 -> 2. PRIOR: module-rand:38 (carrier-receiver closure-word) compiles: `def r (Rand.with-seed N)  r.list-of [Rand.int 0 10] 3` lowers to PUSH_CLOSURE (the generator body re-runs per iteration over the runtime receiver), like the direct `Rand.list-of` form — NOT a frozen rng draw (the sound RecordMakeList freeze-gate is never crossed). with-seed's check-mode ReturnsFn surfaces only the static method SHAPE (a concrete shaped instance Map, identical across seeds — no rng baked); getNodeReturns folds a closure-bearing wrapper field (isClosureBearingWrapper) to the concrete wrapper so it dispatches the closure-word path. An rng-BOUND body (`r.int`) stays dynamic on the interpreter-faithful path. Compiled==interpreter byte-identical across 8 seeds (TestSpecCompiledDifferential + TestRandCarrierReceiverClosureCompiles). 4 -> 3. module-parselang:23 (boru-registered parser to bytecode) compiles: ParseLang.register installs parse_<name> at CHECK time (a check-mode ReturnsFn on parselang-register) so ParseLang.parse_<name> resolves statically, AND the resolved parser fn VALUE — called with args — lowers to a CALL_USER unit instead of leaking __pa into the residual (spliceFnValueCheckResult routes a non-anonymous body-bearing fn value through buildFnBodyReturnsFn → StartFnCompile, arming the __pa tail inside its own unit, exactly like a named fn; engine.go). The runtime register handler is idempotent on the fn-value source Pos so the compiled program's re-run of the SAME register is a no-op (a genuine double-register still errors parse_kind_exists). Compiled==interpreter byte-identical (TestSpecCompiledDifferential). Precision cost: a few fn-value calls under carrier args now emit body diagnostics (pinnedFalsePositives settles at 114) — sound, documented. 5 -> 4. surface-shape typed dispatch compiles (surface.tsv:32, `… def total fn [[s:Shape] [Float] [area s]] … total (make Circle {r:4.0})` -> 8.0, fully native): the construction-time body check (checkFnBodyAtConstruction) analyses every fn body against its DECLARED param types — for an abstract surface param (`s:Shape`) the body's `area s` takes the surface-shape dispatch path and calls MarkUncompilable, and the check used Suspend, which stops recording but does NOT stop MarkUncompilable from latching the program's EmitState.Compilable. So a fn with a surface param wrongly marked the WHOLE program uncompilable. Fixed by running the construction check under CheckState.IsolateEmit (a throwaway EmitState, like the help eval) instead of Suspend, so its MarkUncompilable/recording land on the discarded state while its diagnostics (uncalled-body typo etc.) still emit on r.Check.Diagnostics; the real call-site analysis (s bound to the concrete Circle) then compiles `area s` natively. 6 -> 5. dynamic-help eval made FULLY HERMETIC (CheckState.IsolateEmit): the example generator fires from OnRegisterHook on EVERY fn registration — INCLUDING the program's own `def f fn […]` DURING compilation — and Suspend kept the SAME EmitState, so the example's interned consts / RememberOriginal (e.g. a generated `['a' 'b']` sample list for a list-typed param) LEAKED into the program's own pool, corrupting a later operand's compile (a valid `def zs [1 2] end  f2 zs` saw f2's `[:Integer]` arg as the stale `['a' 'b']` and refused). IsolateEmit swaps a throwaway EmitState for the eval (discarded on restore), plus the diagnostic snapshot (decision.boru 39 phantom errors -> 0, from the synthetic {a:1,b:2} stand-in args) and a Defs snapshot. Its accidental construction-time-body-check side-channel was replaced by a FIRST-CLASS pass (checkFnBodyAtConstruction, carrier args, post-binding). Net effect on the count: the decouple transiently EXPOSED 4 previously-check-error rows as refusals (6 -> 9); 3 of them (generics-fn.tsv:43/56 typed-list dispatch, macro.tsv:26 macro-def) were FALSE refusals from this very contamination and now COMPILE; recursion.tsv:72 (dynamic scope) also compiles; only surface.tsv:32 (surface-shape dispatch) is a genuine remaining gap — so the count returns to 6 while decision.boru is fixed and a real latent compile-corruption bug is closed (9 -> 6). PRIOR 7 -> 6: returned capturing closure + per-iteration dynamic apply (bytecode-combinations.tsv:74, `def mk2 fn [[x:Integer] [Function] [([y:Integer] => [x add y])]] for 3 [(mk2 i) 10]` -> `10 11 12`) compiled VM-native — tryReturnedClosure threads a capturing returned lambda's captures before OpPushClosure, and RecordLoop.setLoopBodyApply + lowerFragment emit a per-iteration OpCallDynamic over the leading fn carrier. set-over-dynamic-receiver poly: `set` joins get/getr in tryRecordPoly's QuoteArgs exemption (its atom key bakes as an inert const), so a `set` over a dynamic carrier records OpCallNativePoly instead of refusing "dynamic input at set". callPoly re-matches over the concrete runtime receiver and runs the same handler — the Store/Object/Array mutation and the Map/List copy-return are faithful (differential-clean over the post-mutation observable). Cleared module-io.tsv:39/40 (`context get __sys get fs set mem true  IO.write …` -> `[mem://b.txt]`; the IO.read of the written path -> `[hello]`), eliminating the "dynamic input" refusal category (2 -> 0); 2556 -> 2558 native, 9 -> 7. 0-output dynamic poly: a side-effect / list word whose check-mode result carries NO value (`drop` over a dynamic list, the framework's 0-output `test-record`) reaching a pure builtin matchSignature cannot bind is now a 0-result poly candidate — RecordPolyCall/tryRecordPoly admit len(outs)<=1, OpCallNativePoly pops the operands and the VM pushes nothing, and the recovery threads the dispatching registry (e.registry) into PolyRef.Reg so a module sub-registry word re-matches over its own registry. Cleared flex.tsv:138 (`def l [1 2] push 3 l drop l` -> `[1 2]`), 0 divergences 10 -> 9 (dynamic-input refusals 3 -> 2). STAGE A — multi-value / variadic branch-result modeling (`def m fn [[n:Integer] [] [if (n lte 0) [] [n mul 2 m (n sub 1)]]] m 3` -> `6 4 2`, recursion.tsv:53). A `[]`-declared fn whose else arm leaves a FIXED value (`n*2`) below a runtime-VARIABLE tail (the recursive `m` result) refused "branch leaves extra values": the lowerer required single-result arms, the recorder skipped the body-recording refinement re-run (leaving multiple residuals), and reconcileResults rejected a variadic body return. The interpreter leaves a multi-value arm's WHOLE residual (`if true [1 2 3] [4]` -> 1 2 3), so a mismatched/multi-value `if` is genuinely variadic. Five coordinated changes (no VM opcode change — OpRet already leaves everything above stackBase, checkReturnContract no-ops for []-returns): (1) lowerFragment accepts a multi-value arm when the lowered sim count equals the RECORDED carrier residual (EmitFragment.residualN) — a genuine 2-value arm compiles, a single-value expression whose lowering leaves an extra sim slot (`[({a:(get…)} get a)]` records 1) still refuses, and an inert-tail multi arm (`[1 2 3]`, only the top operand recorded) refuses; (2) lowerArms/lowerBranch force the merge variadic via fragMulti; (3) AnalyseFnBody skips the refinement re-run when ARMED (a []-declared recursive fn's summary needs no type sharpening), giving one clean recording; (4) reconcileResults accepts a variadic TAIL for a no-contract ([]-declared) fn; (5) a VARIADIC-RETURNING fn (eventFlags.variadicResult on its body's defining branch/loop, fnUnitRec.variadic) marks its call result variadic at lowerUserCall, so layoutOperands refuses it as a fixed-arity operand — the soundness gate keeping `m 3 add 1` / `f 3 add 1` a refusal (verified: a variadic result fed to add diverged internal_error vs signature_error before the gate). markTailCalls' fragSingleResidual guard stops mis-marking the non-tail recursive call (n*2 below it). Cleared recursion.tsv:53, 0 divergences (property fuzz caught and drove out two unsound intermediates: the get-a artifact and the const-cond multi arm) 11 -> 10. user-call result above a literal in the program residual: a residual `[literal, user-call-result]` (`def add2 fn […] 1 add2 vs` where `vs` splices 2 3 → residual [1, 5]) refused "residual shape beyond Stage 1 (call result above a literal)" because planValueDefLocals promoted only evCall (native) results to a frame local for the out-of-order forceOrder seat, never evCallUser. Extended the forceOrder promotion to user-fn calls (evCallUser, nout==1) — store-once + re-push-in-order — and lowerUserCall now emits OpStoreLocal when promoted (lower.go). Narrowed to forceOrder ONLY (not refs>=2/valueDef/dead) for user calls: a user call may feed a harness/accumulation the residual ref count doesn't capture (Test.run-spec diverged when promoted broadly). Cleared the word-splice row 21 -> 20 (also reducibleCeiling 3 -> 2). mini/parse expansion-time error traps: `mini re 'a'`, `+re/[a-z]+/` (mini_unknown_lang) and `parse calc 'x'` (parse_unknown_lang) use a core word whose kind needs an import that is absent. The checker is lenient (the import "may be outside the checked fragment", so miniHandler/parseHandler degrade to a dynamic carrier — native_macro.go) but the interpreter raises at the call site. A top-level RecordTrap in that lenient branch now compiles a TERMINAL OpTrap raising the byte-identical *_unknown_lang; a nested call declines and keeps the dynamic-carrier fallback. Cleared the 3 no-import minilang/parselang rows 24 -> 21. (MathUtil!.nope not_found stays refused — getr-on-ModuleExport doesn't compile even for a VALID key, so the trap can't win over that independent provenance refusal; deferred to the getr-residual work.) illegal_ref trap programs: `def x 5  x/v` and `def x 5  x/u` apply a ref-family modifier (`/v`/`/u`) to a NON-fn binding. The checker is lenient here (the illegal_ref diagnostic defaults to SeverityInfo, advisory), but the interpreter raises illegal_ref at runtime, so the rows refused on the downstream Undefined placeholder ("operand provenance"). A top-level RecordTrap at the two `!IsFunctionRef(v)` check-active branches (engine.go stepWord `/v` + stepWordUsurp `/u`) now compiles a TERMINAL OpTrap raising the byte-identical illegal_ref instead of refusing; a nested `/v`/`/u` (RecordTrap declines off the top frame) keeps the placeholder path and falls back as before. Cleared the ref.tsv + usurp.tsv illegal_ref rows 26 -> 24 (the x/u row also left tier-2 usurp, reducibleCeiling 4 -> 3). codequote (code-as-data): a Quoted ParenExpr (`codequote (1 add 2)` → `paren([1 word(add) 2])`) now bakes as an inert const — stepLiteral leaves a Quoted ParenExpr unevaluated and the VM never re-steps a const, so it pushes verbatim like the macroexpand token list (isInertConst ParenExpr case + isInertQuotedParen, emit.go); cleared the 2 codequote.tsv rows 28 -> 26. make with a computed construction body inside a fn body: `make (Box of [T]) {value:x}` (x a param) refused because RecordMakeMap was top-frame-only. But make's body is a CONSUMED arg (dataMap, match.Name=="make") it evaluates in the current scope — never a deferred residual — so the OpMakeMap re-assembles per call soundly; dropped the top-frame gate for the dataMap case (engine.go autoEvalMap + RecordMakeMap). Cleared 3 generics-fn.tsv rows (boxit/boxfirst/pairup) 31 -> 28. dispatch-modifier word forms: forward-args / stack-args / force-arity now bake like usurp — their re-dispatch wrappers (rebarrierFunction / ForceArityFunction, core_ref.go) gained RunInCheckMode, so the carrier compiler steps the re-dispatch and compiles the underlying call directly (`forward-args sub 10 3` lowers like `sub`), instead of refusing the opaque wrapper; cleared 4 path-modifier.tsv rows 35 -> 31. await: a code-body native with a dynamic (declared-Any) result now bakes a plain CALL_NATIVE — dynOutNativeOK admits an inert NoEvalArgs body (the same noEvalBodiesInert screen RecordCall uses) instead of a blanket NoEvalArgs exclusion, so the handler runs its parallels faithfully and the dynamic result bakes; cleared the module-time.tsv await row 36 -> 35. cross-fn break/continue: a break/continue in a FN body with no enclosing loop in its own unit now compiles — the recorder emits OpFlowBreak/OpFlowContinue (lower.go) instead of refusing, and the VM unwinds to the nearest open loop in an ANCESTOR frame (vmLoop's exitPC/nextPC/unit/iterBase, derived from the instruction stream), discarding the current iteration's partial like the interpreter's mark→move splice. Two enablers landed alongside: a 0-value `if cond [break] []` (both arms net 0) now records zeroOut instead of refusing "branches produce no value" (native_control.go if3 + eng RecordBranch), and AnalyseFnBody strips the zeroOut phantom None from a fn's CALL residual so a 0-return fn (`def b fn [[n][][if … [break] []]]`) nets 0 to a multi-value caller (`b i i`). Cleared the 2 recursion.tsv §9 cross-fn break/continue rows, 0 divergences 38 -> 36. empty-body user fn returning ZERO values (`def v fn [[x:Integer] [] []]`): the check-mode ReturnsFn returned a bogus single Any approximation and skipped recording, so `v 1 99` (→ [99]) and `v 1` (→ []) refused "user fn call (Stage 3)". When the body unit compiles, it now records a 0-output CALL_USER and returns zero carriers — matching runtime exactly (the call runs for effects, the next token is the residual). recordCallElided gained the 0-output user-fn case (unambiguous: a DECLINED unit still returns the 1-value Any approximation, so only the compiled call reaches with an empty residual). The two error consumers (`def r (f 1)` → def_error, `add 5 (f 1)` → no_value_error) now surface their diagnostic at check time and fall back, faithfully 40 -> 38. reach computed-segment + TimeUtil timeout/interval body: (a) isInertConstMember now admits a deferred ParenExpr (`reach 5 [a (add 1 2) c]`) riding inside a never-evaluated compound — like the Word/Reach member cases it is pure data the reach handler re-runs at apply time, so the key list bakes as a const + CALL_NATIVE; cleared the 2 reach computed-segment code-body rows. (b) The inner timeout/interval LIST sigs gained NoEvalArgs (alongside QuoteArgs): the execMatch auto-eval gate keys ONLY off NoEvalArgs, and the module wrapper's trivial delegation runs execMatch on the inner sig, so without it `TimeUtil.timeout 1000 [body]` sub-Ran the body eagerly (a latent runtime bug — `[print 9]` printed at construction) AND reached the recorder as the evaluated type `[Integer]`, unbakeable. With NoEvalArgs the body stays raw code, noEvalBodiesInert bakes it, and the QuoteArgs refusal (hasUncoveredQuoteArg) no longer double-refuses a position already covered by NoEvalArgs; cleared the 3 TimeUtil quoted-operand rows + 1 operand-provenance row 46 -> 40. dynamic input to a closure-dispatch word: RecordClosureCall pre-declined ANY dynamic non-body operand, but the loop below already declines via resolveOperand when one genuinely cannot resolve. A caught `do` error reaching `error [handler]` is a resolvable event whose closure input is a FIXED TError carrier (independent of the dynamic value — the handler runs faithfully over whatever it turns out to be), so the pre-decline was overly conservative. Dropping it (relying on resolveOperand) compiles `do [{x:1} !. y] error [get code